 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Worn down by his colleague, the finance employee finally agrees to step into the marketing meeting. Right as they settle the ground rules outside the door, an organizer walks out with breaking news. Listen in Brazilian Portuguese first, and do not worry about catching every word.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "Tá bom. Você vai entrar lá dentro.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "E você vai ficar sentado esse tempo inteiro.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "Fechou? Estamos alinhados?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "financeiro",
   "t": "Sim, senhor.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "organizador",
   "t": "Ih, gente! O cara desmarcou a reunião.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "Desmarcou? Que que houve?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "organizador",
   "t": "A outra reunião atrasou. Ele não vem mais.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "organizador",
   "t": "Estamos livres!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "Estamos livres! Vamos tomar um cafezinho?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "financeiro",
   "t": "Eu gostaria, mas agora tenho que resolver umas coisinhas.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "Vamos tomar um cafezinho com adoçante!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "financeiro",
   "t": "Oba.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Let's go back through the twist one line at a time.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "Tá bom. Você vai entrar lá dentro.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'All right. You're going inside there.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "financeiro",
   "t": "Tá bom.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", literally \"it's good\", is the everyday Brazilian \"okay\" or \"all right\", though here the colleague uses it to start giving orders.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "E você vai ficar sentado esse tempo inteiro.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And you're going to stay seated that whole time.' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "inteiro.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means \"whole\" or \"entire\", and placing it after ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "esse tempo",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " stresses every single minute he has to sit still.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "Fechou? Estamos alinhados?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Deal? Are we aligned?' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "Fechou?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally means \"it closed\", like closing a deal, and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "estamos alinhados?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is classic Brazilian office-speak for \"we're on the same page\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "financeiro",
   "t": "Sim, senhor.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, sir.' Calling a peer ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "financeiro",
   "t": "senhor.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " here is pure dry humour, like saluting a drill sergeant just to end the lecture.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "organizador",
   "t": "Ih, gente! O cara desmarcou a reunião.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Oh, folks! The guy canceled the meeting.' The verb means to book or schedule, so adding a prefix flips ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "desmarcou",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " neatly into \"un-booked\" or \"canceled\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "Desmarcou? Que que houve?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'He canceled? What happened?' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "financeiro",
   "t": "Que que houve?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the go-to spoken question when something unexpected happens, built around the verb meaning \"there was\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "organizador",
   "t": "A outra reunião atrasou. Ele não vem mais.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'The other meeting ran late. He is not coming anymore.' Adding ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "mais.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " right after ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "não vem",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " gives you \"not anymore\", where ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "mais.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " literally means \"more\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "organizador",
   "t": "Estamos livres!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We are free!' Because ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "livres!",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " describes both of them together, it adds an 's' to match ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "estamos",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning \"we are\".",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "Estamos livres! Vamos tomar um cafezinho?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We are free! Shall we grab a little coffee?' In Brazil you never just invite someone for the plain drink: adding the warm ending makes ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "cafezinho?",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " sound quick, friendly, and impossible to refuse.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "financeiro",
   "t": "Eu gostaria, mas agora tenho que resolver umas coisinhas.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I would like to, but right now I have to sort out a few little things.' Notice the employee fights back with the same affectionate ending: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "financeiro",
   "t": "coisinhas.",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " shrinks \"things\" into \"just a few little tasks\" to soften his excuse.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "Vamos tomar um cafezinho com adoçante!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Let's have a little coffee with sweetener!' ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "colega",
   "t": "adoçante!",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " comes from a root meaning \"sweet\", so it literally names the thing that sweetens your cup.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "financeiro",
   "t": "Oba.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yay.' Normally ",
